{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-346",
              "name": "Missing origin and state validation on OAuth postMessage handler in ConnectorPanel",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Missing origin and state validation on OAuth postMessage handler in ConnectorPanel"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The OAuth callback message listener in the SPA accepts any 'oauth-callback' postMessage without validating event.origin or a state nonce, so any window holding a reference to the SPA can deliver a forged callback and bind the victim's connector to an attacker-chosen credentialId."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The OAuth callback message listener in the SPA accepts any 'oauth-callback' postMessage without validating event.origin or a state nonce, so any window holding a reference to the SPA can deliver a forged callback and bind the victim's connector to an attacker-chosen credentialId.\n\nAn attacker page that holds a window reference to the SPA (e.g. an opener, or an adjacent attacker-controlled page) can send a forged `{type:'oauth-callback', payload:{success:true, credentialId:<attacker value>}}` message while a victim initiates a connector OAuth flow. The victim's connector is then configured with the attacker's credentialId, so subsequent agent actions using that connector authenticate with attacker-controlled credentials — a credential-confusion outcome where the attacker acts under the victim's context or observes traffic routed through their credential.\n\nValidate `event.origin` against the expected API/popup origin and verify a per-flow state nonce echoed in the message payload (generate a random state before opening the popup, require it in the callback, and reject mismatches). Also confirm `event.source` is the expected popup window.",
                "markdown": "The OAuth callback message listener in the SPA accepts any 'oauth-callback' postMessage without validating event.origin or a state nonce, so any window holding a reference to the SPA can deliver a forged callback and bind the victim's connector to an attacker-chosen credentialId.\n\nAn attacker page that holds a window reference to the SPA (e.g. an opener, or an adjacent attacker-controlled page) can send a forged `{type:'oauth-callback', payload:{success:true, credentialId:<attacker value>}}` message while a victim initiates a connector OAuth flow. The victim's connector is then configured with the attacker's credentialId, so subsequent agent actions using that connector authenticate with attacker-controlled credentials — a credential-confusion outcome where the attacker acts under the victim's context or observes traffic routed through their credential.\n\nValidate `event.origin` against the expected API/popup origin and verify a per-flow state nonce echoed in the message payload (generate a random state before opening the popup, require it in the callback, and reject mismatches). Also confirm `event.source` is the expected popup window."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.2",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-346",
                  "stride:S"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/346.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-598",
              "name": "Session token passed in URL query string across preview endpoints",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Session token passed in URL query string across preview endpoints"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The SPA appends the user's full authentication token as a `?token=` query parameter on preview, PDF, published-app, and static-asset URLs, and the backend explicitly accepts query-string tokens. Session tokens therefore leak into browser history, proxy/server access logs, and Referer headers."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The SPA appends the user's full authentication token as a `?token=` query parameter on preview, PDF, published-app, and static-asset URLs, and the backend explicitly accepts query-string tokens. Session tokens therefore leak into browser history, proxy/server access logs, and Referer headers.\n\nThe user's session token (Cognito ID token or local auth token) is embedded in URLs that are stored in browser history, recorded in web-server and proxy access logs, and can be leaked via Referer headers if such a URL is embedded on a third-party page. Anyone with access to those channels obtains the victim's full session token and can act as the victim against the API until token expiry. No confidentiality/integrity impact is demonstrated without log/history access — this is a token-exposure weakness.\n\nReplace query-string tokens with short-lived, single-purpose, HttpOnly-cookie or one-time preview tickets minted for the specific resource URL (e.g. signed download URLs with expiry), and remove `?token=` acceptance from the backend middleware. Where iframe rendering is unavoidable, use a POST-issued temporary cookie or a per-session preview token distinct from the primary session token.",
                "markdown": "The SPA appends the user's full authentication token as a `?token=` query parameter on preview, PDF, published-app, and static-asset URLs, and the backend explicitly accepts query-string tokens. Session tokens therefore leak into browser history, proxy/server access logs, and Referer headers.\n\nThe user's session token (Cognito ID token or local auth token) is embedded in URLs that are stored in browser history, recorded in web-server and proxy access logs, and can be leaked via Referer headers if such a URL is embedded on a third-party page. Anyone with access to those channels obtains the victim's full session token and can act as the victim against the API until token expiry. No confidentiality/integrity impact is demonstrated without log/history access — this is a token-exposure weakness.\n\nReplace query-string tokens with short-lived, single-purpose, HttpOnly-cookie or one-time preview tickets minted for the specific resource URL (e.g. signed download URLs with expiry), and remove `?token=` acceptance from the backend middleware. Where iframe rendering is unavoidable, use a POST-issued temporary cookie or a per-session preview token distinct from the primary session token."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.7",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-598",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/598.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-792",
              "name": "Preview iframes combine allow-scripts with allow-same-origin, nullifying sandbox isolation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Preview iframes combine allow-scripts with allow-same-origin, nullifying sandbox isolation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Agent-output and published-app preview iframes use sandbox=\"allow-scripts allow-same-origin allow-forms allow-popups\". For content served from the SPA/API origin this combination nullifies the sandbox: framed scripts run with full same-origin privileges and can read the parent's localStorage session tokens, which are also handed to the frame via ?token=."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Agent-output and published-app preview iframes use sandbox=\"allow-scripts allow-same-origin allow-forms allow-popups\". For content served from the SPA/API origin this combination nullifies the sandbox: framed scripts run with full same-origin privileges and can read the parent's localStorage session tokens, which are also handed to the frame via ?token=.\n\nA user-authored published app or agent-generated HTML/JS file previewed in one of these iframes executes script in the API/SPA origin (when co-hosted). It can read `localStorage.local_auth_token` / `cognito_id_token` of the victim — including via the token already present in its own URL — and exfiltrate the victim's session, or issue credentialed API requests. Any agent-authored content (which can incorporate untrusted instructions from knowledge-base files or prompt injection) becomes a same-origin script execution vector.\n\nServe previewed app/file content from a dedicated origin (separate subdomain) and drop `allow-same-origin` from the sandbox attribute, keeping `allow-scripts allow-forms`. Replace URL tokens with per-resource signed preview URLs or a scoped cookie, so the frame never receives or inherits the primary session token.",
                "markdown": "Agent-output and published-app preview iframes use sandbox=\"allow-scripts allow-same-origin allow-forms allow-popups\". For content served from the SPA/API origin this combination nullifies the sandbox: framed scripts run with full same-origin privileges and can read the parent's localStorage session tokens, which are also handed to the frame via ?token=.\n\nA user-authored published app or agent-generated HTML/JS file previewed in one of these iframes executes script in the API/SPA origin (when co-hosted). It can read `localStorage.local_auth_token` / `cognito_id_token` of the victim — including via the token already present in its own URL — and exfiltrate the victim's session, or issue credentialed API requests. Any agent-authored content (which can incorporate untrusted instructions from knowledge-base files or prompt injection) becomes a same-origin script execution vector.\n\nServe previewed app/file content from a dedicated origin (separate subdomain) and drop `allow-same-origin` from the sandbox attribute, keeping `allow-scripts allow-forms`. Replace URL tokens with per-resource signed preview URLs or a scoped cookie, so the frame never receives or inherits the primary session token."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.2",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-792",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/792.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "Cross-organization IDOR on knowledge-base, briefing, and scope plugin/MCP-server routes (missing organization_id scoping)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cross-organization IDOR on knowledge-base, briefing, and scope plugin/MCP-server routes (missing organization_id scoping)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Knowledge-base, briefing, and business-scope plugin/MCP-server routes in the backend Fastify API perform object lookups solely by primary-key identifier with no organization scoping. Any authenticated user of any tenant can read, modify, or soft-delete another organization's knowledge bases, files, folders, and briefings by supplying the target object's UUID."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Knowledge-base, briefing, and business-scope plugin/MCP-server routes in the backend Fastify API perform object lookups solely by primary-key identifier with no organization scoping. Any authenticated user of any tenant can read, modify, or soft-delete another organization's knowledge bases, files, folders, and briefings by supplying the target object's UUID.\n\nCross-tenant (cross-organization) read, modification, and deletion of tenant data: full knowledge-base metadata, file listings and metadata, and scope briefings of any organization; soft-deleting or renaming another org's knowledge bases; mutating their files. This breaks the multi-tenant isolation boundary of the platform for an entire resource family.\n\nAdd an organization scoping predicate to every object lookup on these route families. For the knowledge-base service, change `findById`, `update`, `delete`, `listFiles`, `updateFile`, `deleteFile`, `renameFolder`, `deleteFolder`, and the batch operations to accept and filter on `organization_id` (e.g. `findUnique` → `findFirst({ where: { id, organization_id } })`), and pass `request.user.orgId` from every route handler. For briefing and business-scope routes, first resolve the business scope by `id` AND `organization_id`, returning 404 when it is not the caller's tenant, before querying or mutating child resources (briefings, plugins, mcp-servers). Do not rely on role checks (`requireModifyAccess`) as a tenant boundary.",
                "markdown": "Knowledge-base, briefing, and business-scope plugin/MCP-server routes in the backend Fastify API perform object lookups solely by primary-key identifier with no organization scoping. Any authenticated user of any tenant can read, modify, or soft-delete another organization's knowledge bases, files, folders, and briefings by supplying the target object's UUID.\n\nCross-tenant (cross-organization) read, modification, and deletion of tenant data: full knowledge-base metadata, file listings and metadata, and scope briefings of any organization; soft-deleting or renaming another org's knowledge bases; mutating their files. This breaks the multi-tenant isolation boundary of the platform for an entire resource family.\n\nAdd an organization scoping predicate to every object lookup on these route families. For the knowledge-base service, change `findById`, `update`, `delete`, `listFiles`, `updateFile`, `deleteFile`, `renameFolder`, `deleteFolder`, and the batch operations to accept and filter on `organization_id` (e.g. `findUnique` → `findFirst({ where: { id, organization_id } })`), and pass `request.user.orgId` from every route handler. For briefing and business-scope routes, first resolve the business scope by `id` AND `organization_id`, returning 404 when it is not the caller's tenant, before querying or mutating child resources (briefings, plugins, mcp-servers). Do not rely on role checks (`requireModifyAccess`) as a tenant boundary."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-798",
              "name": "Hardcoded default admin password 'Admin1234!' seeded and logged by deployment scripts",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Hardcoded default admin password 'Admin1234!' seeded and logged by deployment scripts"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The deployment scripts seed a default administrator account (admin@example.com / Admin1234!) on every fresh deployment, never force a password change, and echo the plaintext credential into deployment logs. Because the credential is also published in the repository's deployment documentation, anyone who can reach a deployment's login endpoint can gain full admin access on any instance where the operator did not manually change the password."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The deployment scripts seed a default administrator account (admin@example.com / Admin1234!) on every fresh deployment, never force a password change, and echo the plaintext credential into deployment logs. Because the credential is also published in the repository's deployment documentation, anyone who can reach a deployment's login endpoint can gain full admin access on any instance where the operator did not manually change the password.\n\nFull administrative takeover of any deployment provisioned by these scripts where the default was not changed: the admin account can read and modify all tenant data, agents, workflows, and platform configuration via the backend API. Deploy-log readers (CI artifacts, SSH session transcripts) obtain the credential even if the operator never read the docs.\n\nGenerate a random admin password per deployment and deliver it through a secure channel (or require first-boot rotation), never echo credentials to logs, and do not publish default credentials in documentation. Block authentication on the seeded admin account until the password is changed.",
                "markdown": "The deployment scripts seed a default administrator account (admin@example.com / Admin1234!) on every fresh deployment, never force a password change, and echo the plaintext credential into deployment logs. Because the credential is also published in the repository's deployment documentation, anyone who can reach a deployment's login endpoint can gain full admin access on any instance where the operator did not manually change the password.\n\nFull administrative takeover of any deployment provisioned by these scripts where the default was not changed: the admin account can read and modify all tenant data, agents, workflows, and platform configuration via the backend API. Deploy-log readers (CI artifacts, SSH session transcripts) obtain the credential even if the operator never read the docs.\n\nGenerate a random admin password per deployment and deliver it through a secure channel (or require first-boot rotation), never echo credentials to logs, and do not publish default credentials in documentation. Block authentication on the seeded admin account until the password is changed."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-798",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-287",
              "name": "Authentication forgery family: internal-token HMAC key publicly disclosed, default JWT secret, and query-parameter token acceptance",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Authentication forgery family: internal-token HMAC key publicly disclosed, default JWT secret, and query-parameter token acceptance"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The backend's authentication middleware (middleware/auth.ts) can be satisfied by three separately forgeable token classes: (a) JWTs signed with the hard-coded default secret `super-agent-local-dev-secret-change-in-production` that config applies whenever JWT_SECRET is unset in local auth mode; (b) \"internal service tokens\" whose HMAC key in Cognito mode is derived from the Cognito userPoolId — a value the backend itself discloses unauthenticated at GET /api/auth/config; (c) JWTs passed as a `?token=` query parameter on any route, which leak into logs, referrers, and proxy histories. Each allows impersonating any user, role, and organization."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The backend's authentication middleware (middleware/auth.ts) can be satisfied by three separately forgeable token classes: (a) JWTs signed with the hard-coded default secret `super-agent-local-dev-secret-change-in-production` that config applies whenever JWT_SECRET is unset in local auth mode; (b) \"internal service tokens\" whose HMAC key in Cognito mode is derived from the Cognito userPoolId — a value the backend itself discloses unauthenticated at GET /api/auth/config; (c) JWTs passed as a `?token=` query parameter on any route, which leak into logs, referrers, and proxy histories. Each allows impersonating any user, role, and organization.\n\nComplete authentication bypass. In Cognito production mode the internal-token path lets an anonymous attacker who fetched /api/auth/config forge a token for any user id, any role (including `owner`), and any organization, bypassing even the profile-existence check that normal tokens undergo. In local mode (or wherever JWT_SECRET is unset) the default secret allows forging arbitrary local JWTs. Query-param tokens additionally expose valid credentials in URLs. Consequence is full cross-tenant account and privilege impersonation across the API.\n\n(1) Give internal service tokens their own high-entropy secret (dedicated environment variable), never derive them from the public userPoolId, and restrict their acceptance to a private network path or an explicit allowlist of internal callers; consider a dedicated Fastify plugin/prefix only reachable internally. (2) Remove the default JWT_SECRET from the zod schema and fail startup when AUTH_MODE=local and JWT_SECRET is missing in non-development environments. (3) Remove query-parameter token acceptance in authenticate(); if iframe/img use cases need it, restrict it to a narrow, explicit allowlist of read-only routes and short-lived tokens, and ensure tokens never appear in logs.",
                "markdown": "The backend's authentication middleware (middleware/auth.ts) can be satisfied by three separately forgeable token classes: (a) JWTs signed with the hard-coded default secret `super-agent-local-dev-secret-change-in-production` that config applies whenever JWT_SECRET is unset in local auth mode; (b) \"internal service tokens\" whose HMAC key in Cognito mode is derived from the Cognito userPoolId — a value the backend itself discloses unauthenticated at GET /api/auth/config; (c) JWTs passed as a `?token=` query parameter on any route, which leak into logs, referrers, and proxy histories. Each allows impersonating any user, role, and organization.\n\nComplete authentication bypass. In Cognito production mode the internal-token path lets an anonymous attacker who fetched /api/auth/config forge a token for any user id, any role (including `owner`), and any organization, bypassing even the profile-existence check that normal tokens undergo. In local mode (or wherever JWT_SECRET is unset) the default secret allows forging arbitrary local JWTs. Query-param tokens additionally expose valid credentials in URLs. Consequence is full cross-tenant account and privilege impersonation across the API.\n\n(1) Give internal service tokens their own high-entropy secret (dedicated environment variable), never derive them from the public userPoolId, and restrict their acceptance to a private network path or an explicit allowlist of internal callers; consider a dedicated Fastify plugin/prefix only reachable internally. (2) Remove the default JWT_SECRET from the zod schema and fail startup when AUTH_MODE=local and JWT_SECRET is missing in non-development environments. (3) Remove query-parameter token acceptance in authenticate(); if iframe/img use cases need it, restrict it to a narrow, explicit allowlist of read-only routes and short-lived tokens, and ensure tokens never appear in logs."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-287",
                  "stride:S"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/287.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-1321",
              "name": "CVE-2023-30533 in xlsx 0.18.5 (prototype pollution parsing agent-generated spreadsheets)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2023-30533 in xlsx 0.18.5 (prototype pollution parsing agent-generated spreadsheets)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The frontend pins `xlsx` (SheetJS) 0.18.5 in `frontend/package.json`, which is affected by CVE-2023-30533 (prototype pollution via crafted worksheet, fixed in 0.19.3). The library parses attacker-supplied spreadsheet files in the chat file preview."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The frontend pins `xlsx` (SheetJS) 0.18.5 in `frontend/package.json`, which is affected by CVE-2023-30533 (prototype pollution via crafted worksheet, fixed in 0.19.3). The library parses attacker-supplied spreadsheet files in the chat file preview.\n\nA crafted .xlsx opened in the chat workspace viewer pollutes `Object.prototype` in the victim's browser tab. The same tab holds the auth token in localStorage, so a pollution-driven gadget in the SPA could be leveraged toward script execution and token theft; at minimum it corrupts application state and can crash the tab.\n\nUpgrade SheetJS to a fixed release (0.19.3 or later via the SheetJS CDN, as the npm `xlsx` package is frozen at 0.18.5), or switch to the maintained `xlsx` distribution source; alternatively replace with another spreadsheet parser. Additionally, validate that any future use of sheet_to_json does not switch to object output (`header` omitted) on untrusted files.",
                "markdown": "The frontend pins `xlsx` (SheetJS) 0.18.5 in `frontend/package.json`, which is affected by CVE-2023-30533 (prototype pollution via crafted worksheet, fixed in 0.19.3). The library parses attacker-supplied spreadsheet files in the chat file preview.\n\nA crafted .xlsx opened in the chat workspace viewer pollutes `Object.prototype` in the victim's browser tab. The same tab holds the auth token in localStorage, so a pollution-driven gadget in the SPA could be leveraged toward script execution and token theft; at minimum it corrupts application state and can crash the tab.\n\nUpgrade SheetJS to a fixed release (0.19.3 or later via the SheetJS CDN, as the npm `xlsx` package is frozen at 0.18.5), or switch to the maintained `xlsx` distribution source; alternatively replace with another spreadsheet parser. Additionally, validate that any future use of sheet_to_json does not switch to object output (`header` omitted) on untrusted files."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.9",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-1321",
                  "CVE-2023-30533",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1321.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-306",
              "name": "Unauthenticated webhook trigger endpoint allows arbitrary tenant workflow execution (no auth, no HMAC, no replay/rate limit)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated webhook trigger endpoint allows arbitrary tenant workflow execution (no auth, no HMAC, no replay/rate limit)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The webhook trigger endpoint POST /v1/webhook/:webhookId/trigger requires no authentication, no signature, and has no replay or rate protection. Anyone who obtains a webhook_id can invoke the victim organization's workflow arbitrarily many times, consuming LLM/agent execution resources and driving unauthorized state changes."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The webhook trigger endpoint POST /v1/webhook/:webhookId/trigger requires no authentication, no signature, and has no replay or rate protection. Anyone who obtains a webhook_id can invoke the victim organization's workflow arbitrarily many times, consuming LLM/agent execution resources and driving unauthorized state changes.\n\nUnauthenticated invocation of tenant workflows/agents on demand: attacker-controlled variables flow into agent execution (prompt-injection surface), resource consumption/cost abuse (each trigger runs the workflow, e.g. Claude/Bedrock calls), and record flooding of the victim org's webhook call history. Integrity impact is unauthorized execution of business logic as the tenant; no direct confidentiality impact was demonstrated.\n\nRequire an authentication/authorization check on the trigger route, or implement a per-webhook secret verified as an HMAC signature over the raw body with a timestamp window (as the IM adapters already do for Slack/Telegram/Discord), plus replay protection and a per-webhook/per-IP rate limit. Log and alert on triggers from unlisted sources.",
                "markdown": "The webhook trigger endpoint POST /v1/webhook/:webhookId/trigger requires no authentication, no signature, and has no replay or rate protection. Anyone who obtains a webhook_id can invoke the victim organization's workflow arbitrarily many times, consuming LLM/agent execution resources and driving unauthorized state changes.\n\nUnauthenticated invocation of tenant workflows/agents on demand: attacker-controlled variables flow into agent execution (prompt-injection surface), resource consumption/cost abuse (each trigger runs the workflow, e.g. Claude/Bedrock calls), and record flooding of the victim org's webhook call history. Integrity impact is unauthorized execution of business logic as the tenant; no direct confidentiality impact was demonstrated.\n\nRequire an authentication/authorization check on the trigger route, or implement a per-webhook secret verified as an HMAC signature over the raw body with a timestamp window (as the IM adapters already do for Slack/Telegram/Discord), plus replay protection and a per-webhook/per-IP rate limit. Log and alert on triggers from unlisted sources."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.2",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-1333",
              "name": "CVE-2024-22363 in xlsx 0.18.5 (ReDoS via crafted spreadsheet parsing)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2024-22363 in xlsx 0.18.5 (ReDoS via crafted spreadsheet parsing)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The frontend pins `xlsx` (SheetJS) 0.18.5, affected by CVE-2024-22363 — regular expression denial of service when parsing crafted spreadsheet content, fixed in 0.20.2. The library parses attacker-supplied Excel files in the chat preview."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The frontend pins `xlsx` (SheetJS) 0.18.5, affected by CVE-2024-22363 — regular expression denial of service when parsing crafted spreadsheet content, fixed in 0.20.2. The library parses attacker-supplied Excel files in the chat preview.\n\nA crafted spreadsheet opened in the chat file preview can cause catastrophic regex backtracking during parsing, freezing the victim's browser tab (client-side denial of service). No confidentiality or integrity impact; no server-side resource consumption since parsing occurs in the browser.\n\nUpgrade SheetJS to 0.20.2 or later (SheetJS CDN distribution; the public npm `xlsx` package is frozen at 0.18.5), or replace with a maintained spreadsheet parser that does not use vulnerable regexes on untrusted input.",
                "markdown": "The frontend pins `xlsx` (SheetJS) 0.18.5, affected by CVE-2024-22363 — regular expression denial of service when parsing crafted spreadsheet content, fixed in 0.20.2. The library parses attacker-supplied Excel files in the chat preview.\n\nA crafted spreadsheet opened in the chat file preview can cause catastrophic regex backtracking during parsing, freezing the victim's browser tab (client-side denial of service). No confidentiality or integrity impact; no server-side resource consumption since parsing occurs in the browser.\n\nUpgrade SheetJS to 0.20.2 or later (SheetJS CDN distribution; the public npm `xlsx` package is frozen at 0.18.5), or replace with a maintained spreadsheet parser that does not use vulnerable regexes on untrusted input."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-1333",
                  "CVE-2024-22363",
                  "stride:D"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1333.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-22",
              "name": "Path traversal in pack-deploy onboarding/deploy via unvalidated packId and scopeDirName",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Path traversal in pack-deploy onboarding/deploy via unvalidated packId and scopeDirName"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The pack onboarding and deploy endpoints pass the raw packId and scopeDirName path parameters into Node path.join() with no validation or containment check, so URL-encoded traversal sequences let an authenticated user read JSON files outside the industry-packs directory tree."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The pack onboarding and deploy endpoints pass the raw packId and scopeDirName path parameters into Node path.join() with no validation or containment check, so URL-encoded traversal sequences let an authenticated user read JSON files outside the industry-packs directory tree.\n\nAuthenticated arbitrary-file read (JSON files only, via the onboarding reader) relative to the process working directory: can disclose configuration files, deployment metadata, or other packs' content present on the server filesystem as JSON. Limited to files that parse as JSON on this path; no write or execute primitive demonstrated.\n\nValidate packId and scopeDirName against a strict format (e.g. ^[a-z0-9-]+$) before any filesystem use, and after constructing each path, verify containment by resolving it and checking that the resolved path remains inside the packs base directory (resolve() + startsWith(baseDir) with a trailing separator), rejecting otherwise. Apply the check in both getOnboardingConfig and deployScope in pack-deploy.service.ts.",
                "markdown": "The pack onboarding and deploy endpoints pass the raw packId and scopeDirName path parameters into Node path.join() with no validation or containment check, so URL-encoded traversal sequences let an authenticated user read JSON files outside the industry-packs directory tree.\n\nAuthenticated arbitrary-file read (JSON files only, via the onboarding reader) relative to the process working directory: can disclose configuration files, deployment metadata, or other packs' content present on the server filesystem as JSON. Limited to files that parse as JSON on this path; no write or execute primitive demonstrated.\n\nValidate packId and scopeDirName against a strict format (e.g. ^[a-z0-9-]+$) before any filesystem use, and after constructing each path, verify containment by resolving it and checking that the resolved path remains inside the packs base directory (resolve() + startsWith(baseDir) with a trailing separator), rejecting otherwise. Apply the check in both getOnboardingConfig and deployScope in pack-deploy.service.ts."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-22",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/22.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-400",
              "name": "CVE-2026-48779 in ws 8.19.0 (WebSocket small-fragment memory exhaustion DoS of the chat gateway)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-48779 in ws 8.19.0 (WebSocket small-fragment memory exhaustion DoS of the chat gateway)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The backend pins `ws` 8.19.0 (pulled by `@fastify/websocket` ^11.2.0) in `backend/package-lock.json`. All `ws` versions from 8.0.0 up to (not including) 8.21.0 are affected by a memory-exhaustion DoS (CVE-2026-48779): a peer can send a high volume of exceptionally small fragments whose structural wrappers consume far more memory than the documented message-size limit, OOM-killing the process. The backend's chat execution WebSocket gateway runs on this version."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The backend pins `ws` 8.19.0 (pulled by `@fastify/websocket` ^11.2.0) in `backend/package-lock.json`. All `ws` versions from 8.0.0 up to (not including) 8.21.0 are affected by a memory-exhaustion DoS (CVE-2026-48779): a peer can send a high volume of exceptionally small fragments whose structural wrappers consume far more memory than the documented message-size limit, OOM-killing the process. The backend's chat execution WebSocket gateway runs on this version.\n\nAn unauthenticated network peer that can open a WebSocket connection to the backend can, with modest traffic, drive the Node process into out-of-memory termination — taking down the API/chat-execution service for all tenants.\n\nIn backend/package.json, force the resolution of `ws` to >= 8.21.0 (npm `overrides: { \"ws\": \"^8.21.0\" }`) or bump `@fastify/websocket` to a release depending on a fixed ws. Verify with `npm ls ws` after the change. The same override should be applied to the frontend lockfile for consistency, though its exposure is client-side only.",
                "markdown": "The backend pins `ws` 8.19.0 (pulled by `@fastify/websocket` ^11.2.0) in `backend/package-lock.json`. All `ws` versions from 8.0.0 up to (not including) 8.21.0 are affected by a memory-exhaustion DoS (CVE-2026-48779): a peer can send a high volume of exceptionally small fragments whose structural wrappers consume far more memory than the documented message-size limit, OOM-killing the process. The backend's chat execution WebSocket gateway runs on this version.\n\nAn unauthenticated network peer that can open a WebSocket connection to the backend can, with modest traffic, drive the Node process into out-of-memory termination — taking down the API/chat-execution service for all tenants.\n\nIn backend/package.json, force the resolution of `ws` to >= 8.21.0 (npm `overrides: { \"ws\": \"^8.21.0\" }`) or bump `@fastify/websocket` to a release depending on a fixed ws. Verify with `npm ls ws` after the change. The same override should be applied to the frontend lockfile for consistency, though its exposure is client-side only."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-400",
                  "CVE-2026-48779",
                  "stride:D"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-183",
              "name": "axios 1.13.6 in backend (multiple 2026 SSRF/proxy-bypass CVEs, not reachable as used)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "axios 1.13.6 in backend (multiple 2026 SSRF/proxy-bypass CVEs, not reachable as used)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The backend pins `axios` 1.13.6 in `backend/package-lock.json`, pulled transitively by the direct IM-adapter SDKs (`@larksuiteoapi/node-sdk` 1.60.0, `@wecom/aibot-node-sdk` 1.0.7, `dingtalk-stream` 2.1.6-beta.1). axios before 1.15.1 is affected by a family of 2026 SSRF/proxy-handling CVEs (e.g. CVE-2026-42043, the 127.0.0.0/8 NO_PROXY bypass that NVD scores 10.0, and CVE-2026-42264, CRLF/header splitting, 9.1). The application code itself imports axios zero times; the only executions are inside the Lark SDK's client against fixed vendor hosts."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The backend pins `axios` 1.13.6 in `backend/package-lock.json`, pulled transitively by the direct IM-adapter SDKs (`@larksuiteoapi/node-sdk` 1.60.0, `@wecom/aibot-node-sdk` 1.0.7, `dingtalk-stream` 2.1.6-beta.1). axios before 1.15.1 is affected by a family of 2026 SSRF/proxy-handling CVEs (e.g. CVE-2026-42043, the 127.0.0.0/8 NO_PROXY bypass that NVD scores 10.0, and CVE-2026-42264, CRLF/header splitting, 9.1). The application code itself imports axios zero times; the only executions are inside the Lark SDK's client against fixed vendor hosts.\n\nAs used here the practical impact is negligible: no application code makes axios requests with attacker-controlled URLs, no NO_PROXY environment is configured, and the SDK targets fixed Feishu/Lark API hosts. The finding documents the supply-chain exposure of shipping an axios version with open CVSS-9/10 proxy-bypass/SSRF advisories; any future code path that passes user-controlled URLs to axios (or a NO_PROXY deployment) would activate them.\n\nAdd an npm override in backend/package.json (\"overrides\": { \"axios\": \"^1.15.2\" }) so all three IM SDK chains resolve a patched axios, and verify with `npm ls axios`. No code change is required — the usage today is vendor-fixed URLs only.",
                "markdown": "The backend pins `axios` 1.13.6 in `backend/package-lock.json`, pulled transitively by the direct IM-adapter SDKs (`@larksuiteoapi/node-sdk` 1.60.0, `@wecom/aibot-node-sdk` 1.0.7, `dingtalk-stream` 2.1.6-beta.1). axios before 1.15.1 is affected by a family of 2026 SSRF/proxy-handling CVEs (e.g. CVE-2026-42043, the 127.0.0.0/8 NO_PROXY bypass that NVD scores 10.0, and CVE-2026-42264, CRLF/header splitting, 9.1). The application code itself imports axios zero times; the only executions are inside the Lark SDK's client against fixed vendor hosts.\n\nAs used here the practical impact is negligible: no application code makes axios requests with attacker-controlled URLs, no NO_PROXY environment is configured, and the SDK targets fixed Feishu/Lark API hosts. The finding documents the supply-chain exposure of shipping an axios version with open CVSS-9/10 proxy-bypass/SSRF advisories; any future code path that passes user-controlled URLs to axios (or a NO_PROXY deployment) would activate them.\n\nAdd an npm override in backend/package.json (\"overrides\": { \"axios\": \"^1.15.2\" }) so all three IM SDK chains resolve a patched axios, and verify with `npm ls axios`. No code change is required — the usage today is vendor-fixed URLs only."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-183",
                  "CVE-2026-42043",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/183.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-94",
              "name": "protobufjs 7.5.4 in backend (CVE-2026-41242 code injection, not reachable as used)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "protobufjs 7.5.4 in backend (CVE-2026-41242 code injection, not reachable as used)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The backend pins `protobufjs` 7.5.4 in `backend/package-lock.json`, pulled transitively by `@larksuiteoapi/node-sdk` 1.60.0 (the Feishu/Lark IM adapter). protobufjs before 7.5.5/8.0.1 is affected by CVE-2026-41242 (CVSS 9.8): code injection through attacker-controlled `type` fields in protobuf definitions executed during object decoding."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The backend pins `protobufjs` 7.5.4 in `backend/package-lock.json`, pulled transitively by `@larksuiteoapi/node-sdk` 1.60.0 (the Feishu/Lark IM adapter). protobufjs before 7.5.5/8.0.1 is affected by CVE-2026-41242 (CVSS 9.8): code injection through attacker-controlled `type` fields in protobuf definitions executed during object decoding.\n\nNo application code path passes attacker-controlled protobuf definitions to protobufjs, so the code-injection primitive is not reachable in this codebase as written. The finding documents that a CVSS-9.8-vulnerable protobufjs ships in the production dependency tree; activation would require a future code path that parses externally supplied .proto definitions.\n\nAdd an npm override in backend/package.json (\"overrides\": { \"protobufjs\": \"^7.5.5\" }) so the @larksuiteoapi/node-sdk chain resolves a patched protobufjs; verify with `npm ls protobufjs`. No code change is required today.",
                "markdown": "The backend pins `protobufjs` 7.5.4 in `backend/package-lock.json`, pulled transitively by `@larksuiteoapi/node-sdk` 1.60.0 (the Feishu/Lark IM adapter). protobufjs before 7.5.5/8.0.1 is affected by CVE-2026-41242 (CVSS 9.8): code injection through attacker-controlled `type` fields in protobuf definitions executed during object decoding.\n\nNo application code path passes attacker-controlled protobuf definitions to protobufjs, so the code-injection primitive is not reachable in this codebase as written. The finding documents that a CVSS-9.8-vulnerable protobufjs ships in the production dependency tree; activation would require a future code path that parses externally supplied .proto definitions.\n\nAdd an npm override in backend/package.json (\"overrides\": { \"protobufjs\": \"^7.5.5\" }) so the @larksuiteoapi/node-sdk chain resolves a patched protobufjs; verify with `npm ls protobufjs`. No code change is required today."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-94",
                  "CVE-2026-41242",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
