            {
              "id": "CWE-502",
              "name": "react-router 7.12.0 in frontend (CVE-2026-42211 and related 2026 CVEs, not reachable in Declarative Mode)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "react-router 7.12.0 in frontend (CVE-2026-42211 and related 2026 CVEs, not reachable in Declarative Mode)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The frontend pins `react-router` 7.12.0 (via direct dependency `react-router-dom` ^7.12.0) in `frontend/package-lock.json`. react-router 7.0.0–7.14.1 is affected by CVE-2026-42211 (advisory CVSS 8.1): in Framework Mode, a multi-step attack leveraging a pre-existing prototype-pollution vulnerability can lead to unauthorized remote code execution. The same pinned version carries additional 2026 advisories (CVE-2026-34077 RSC redirect XSS, CVE-2026-42342 __manifest resource exhaustion) that are likewise confined to Framework/RSC usage."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The frontend pins `react-router` 7.12.0 (via direct dependency `react-router-dom` ^7.12.0) in `frontend/package-lock.json`. react-router 7.0.0–7.14.1 is affected by CVE-2026-42211 (advisory CVSS 8.1): in Framework Mode, a multi-step attack leveraging a pre-existing prototype-pollution vulnerability can lead to unauthorized remote code execution. The same pinned version carries additional 2026 advisories (CVE-2026-34077 RSC redirect XSS, CVE-2026-42342 __manifest resource exhaustion) that are likewise confined to Framework/RSC usage.\n\nThis application uses Declarative Mode (`<BrowserRouter>`), which all three advisories explicitly exclude from impact, so no exploitable path exists as the app is written. The finding documents the vulnerable pinned version; impact would only materialize if the app migrated to Framework Mode / RSC APIs without upgrading.\n\nBump react-router-dom to a release containing the fixes (>= 7.15.0 covers all three advisories; >= 7.14.2 covers CVE-2026-42211) in frontend/package.json and regenerate the lockfile. No code change is required; the Declarative Mode usage is unaffected.",
                "markdown": "The frontend pins `react-router` 7.12.0 (via direct dependency `react-router-dom` ^7.12.0) in `frontend/package-lock.json`. react-router 7.0.0–7.14.1 is affected by CVE-2026-42211 (advisory CVSS 8.1): in Framework Mode, a multi-step attack leveraging a pre-existing prototype-pollution vulnerability can lead to unauthorized remote code execution. The same pinned version carries additional 2026 advisories (CVE-2026-34077 RSC redirect XSS, CVE-2026-42342 __manifest resource exhaustion) that are likewise confined to Framework/RSC usage.\n\nThis application uses Declarative Mode (`<BrowserRouter>`), which all three advisories explicitly exclude from impact, so no exploitable path exists as the app is written. The finding documents the vulnerable pinned version; impact would only materialize if the app migrated to Framework Mode / RSC APIs without upgrading.\n\nBump react-router-dom to a release containing the fixes (>= 7.15.0 covers all three advisories; >= 7.14.2 covers CVE-2026-42211) in frontend/package.json and regenerate the lockfile. No code change is required; the Declarative Mode usage is unaffected."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502",
                  "CVE-2026-42211",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-345",
              "name": "CVE-2026-104850 in @modelcontextprotocol/sdk 1.29.0 (OAuth credential redirection to attacker-chosen MCP server)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-104850 in @modelcontextprotocol/sdk 1.29.0 (OAuth credential redirection to attacker-chosen MCP server)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The agentcore runtime pins `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.29.0 in `agentcore/package-lock.json` (transitive via `@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk` 0.2.141). SDK versions 1.12.0–<1.31.0 are affected by CVE-2026-104850 (advisory CVSS 7.5): the OAuth client support lets the MCP server a client connects to decide which authorization server receives the client's OAuth credentials — stored/pre-provisioned credentials are not bound to their authorization server, so a malicious or compromised MCP server can name its own authorization server in its protected-resource metadata and, without user interaction, receive the client's refresh token."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The agentcore runtime pins `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.29.0 in `agentcore/package-lock.json` (transitive via `@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk` 0.2.141). SDK versions 1.12.0–<1.31.0 are affected by CVE-2026-104850 (advisory CVSS 7.5): the OAuth client support lets the MCP server a client connects to decide which authorization server receives the client's OAuth credentials — stored/pre-provisioned credentials are not bound to their authorization server, so a malicious or compromised MCP server can name its own authorization server in its protected-resource metadata and, without user interaction, receive the client's refresh token.\n\nThe agent runtime forwards tenant-configured `mcp_servers` into the SDK client, so a malicious or prompt-influenced MCP server entry becomes the connected server. If OAuth-based MCP authentication is used in a deployment, that server can capture the client's refresh token / credentials for the platform's authorization server — credential disclosure across a trust boundary to an attacker-controlled endpoint.\n\nUpgrade the MCP SDK to a fixed version (>= 1.31.0, or the 2.2.0 line) by bumping @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk to a release depending on it, or add an override in agentcore/package.json (\"overrides\": { \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk\": \"^1.31.0\" }). Additionally, validate tenant-supplied mcp_servers URLs against an operator allowlist in agent-runner.ts before assigning them to baseOptions.mcpServers.",
                "markdown": "The agentcore runtime pins `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.29.0 in `agentcore/package-lock.json` (transitive via `@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk` 0.2.141). SDK versions 1.12.0–<1.31.0 are affected by CVE-2026-104850 (advisory CVSS 7.5): the OAuth client support lets the MCP server a client connects to decide which authorization server receives the client's OAuth credentials — stored/pre-provisioned credentials are not bound to their authorization server, so a malicious or compromised MCP server can name its own authorization server in its protected-resource metadata and, without user interaction, receive the client's refresh token.\n\nThe agent runtime forwards tenant-configured `mcp_servers` into the SDK client, so a malicious or prompt-influenced MCP server entry becomes the connected server. If OAuth-based MCP authentication is used in a deployment, that server can capture the client's refresh token / credentials for the platform's authorization server — credential disclosure across a trust boundary to an attacker-controlled endpoint.\n\nUpgrade the MCP SDK to a fixed version (>= 1.31.0, or the 2.2.0 line) by bumping @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk to a release depending on it, or add an override in agentcore/package.json (\"overrides\": { \"@modelcontextprotocol/sdk\": \"^1.31.0\" }). Additionally, validate tenant-supplied mcp_servers URLs against an operator allowlist in agent-runner.ts before assigning them to baseOptions.mcpServers."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-345",
                  "CVE-2026-104850",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:T"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/345.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-436",
              "name": "CVE-2026-25223 in fastify 5.6.2 / 4.29.1 (Content-Type tab validation bypass)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-25223 in fastify 5.6.2 / 4.29.1 (Content-Type tab validation bypass)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The backend pins `fastify` 5.6.2 (direct dependency ^5.6.2) in `backend/package-lock.json`. fastify before 5.7.2 is affected by CVE-2026-25223 (advisory CVSS 7.5): request body validation schemas specified by Content-Type can be completely circumvented by appending a tab character followed by arbitrary content to the Content-Type header, while the server still processes the body as the original content type. The same CVE applies to the `app-hosting/poc` services, which pin fastify 4.29.1 (below the 4.x fix in the advisory's range)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The backend pins `fastify` 5.6.2 (direct dependency ^5.6.2) in `backend/package-lock.json`. fastify before 5.7.2 is affected by CVE-2026-25223 (advisory CVSS 7.5): request body validation schemas specified by Content-Type can be completely circumvented by appending a tab character followed by arbitrary content to the Content-Type header, while the server still processes the body as the original content type. The same CVE applies to the `app-hosting/poc` services, which pin fastify 4.29.1 (below the 4.x fix in the advisory's range).\n\nAny route that relies solely on Fastify's Content-Type-based body validation accepts unvalidated bodies. In this codebase most route handlers additionally validate through zod `schema.parse` at handler level, which limits the demonstrated impact to routes whose validation exists only at the Fastify schema layer; the exact set of such routes was not exhaustively enumerated. The bypass is remotely triggerable by any unauthenticated client on any JSON route.\n\nUpgrade fastify to >= 5.7.2 in backend/package.json (e.g. ^5.12.2, which also covers the other 2026 fastify CVEs) and regenerate the lockfile. For the app-hosting/poc services on fastify 4.29.1, upgrade to the fixed 5.x line or accept that the 4.x line no longer receives security patches. Ensure every mutating route enforces a handler-level zod parse so validation does not depend solely on the framework layer.",
                "markdown": "The backend pins `fastify` 5.6.2 (direct dependency ^5.6.2) in `backend/package-lock.json`. fastify before 5.7.2 is affected by CVE-2026-25223 (advisory CVSS 7.5): request body validation schemas specified by Content-Type can be completely circumvented by appending a tab character followed by arbitrary content to the Content-Type header, while the server still processes the body as the original content type. The same CVE applies to the `app-hosting/poc` services, which pin fastify 4.29.1 (below the 4.x fix in the advisory's range).\n\nAny route that relies solely on Fastify's Content-Type-based body validation accepts unvalidated bodies. In this codebase most route handlers additionally validate through zod `schema.parse` at handler level, which limits the demonstrated impact to routes whose validation exists only at the Fastify schema layer; the exact set of such routes was not exhaustively enumerated. The bypass is remotely triggerable by any unauthenticated client on any JSON route.\n\nUpgrade fastify to >= 5.7.2 in backend/package.json (e.g. ^5.12.2, which also covers the other 2026 fastify CVEs) and regenerate the lockfile. For the app-hosting/poc services on fastify 4.29.1, upgrade to the fixed 5.x line or accept that the 4.x line no longer receives security patches. Ensure every mutating route enforces a handler-level zod parse so validation does not depend solely on the framework layer."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-436",
                  "CVE-2026-25223",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/436.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-20",
              "name": "ip-address 10.2.0 in agentcore (CVE-2026-69192 octal host confusion, not applicable as used)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "ip-address 10.2.0 in agentcore (CVE-2026-69192 octal host confusion, not applicable as used)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The agentcore runtime pins `ip-address` 10.2.0 in `agentcore/package-lock.json` (transitive via `@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk` -> `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`). Prior to 10.3.1, `Address4` accepts octets with a leading zero and decodes them as decimal, while the network stack (WHATWG URL parser, inet_aton, getaddrinfo) decodes leading-zero octets as octal — the library and the network stack disagree about which host a string names (CVE-2026-69192, advisory CVSS 8.6). Applications that build trust-boundary decisions (e.g. private-IP / SSRF allowlist checks) on Address4.isPrivate() can be tricked into connecting to a different host than the one validated."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The agentcore runtime pins `ip-address` 10.2.0 in `agentcore/package-lock.json` (transitive via `@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk` -> `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`). Prior to 10.3.1, `Address4` accepts octets with a leading zero and decodes them as decimal, while the network stack (WHATWG URL parser, inet_aton, getaddrinfo) decodes leading-zero octets as octal — the library and the network stack disagree about which host a string names (CVE-2026-69192, advisory CVSS 8.6). Applications that build trust-boundary decisions (e.g. private-IP / SSRF allowlist checks) on Address4.isPrivate() can be tricked into connecting to a different host than the one validated.\n\nNo application code in this repository uses the `ip-address` library, so the octal/decimal confusion has no reachable trust-boundary decision to subvert here. The finding documents the vulnerable pinned version in the agent runtime's dependency tree; it matters only if the SDK (or future code) performs IP-based filtering on attacker-controlled URLs using this library.\n\nAdd an override in agentcore/package.json (\"overrides\": { \"ip-address\": \"^10.3.1\" }) to resolve a patched version in the SDK chain, or track the upstream @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk release that bumps the MCP SDK. No application change is required.",
                "markdown": "The agentcore runtime pins `ip-address` 10.2.0 in `agentcore/package-lock.json` (transitive via `@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk` -> `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`). Prior to 10.3.1, `Address4` accepts octets with a leading zero and decodes them as decimal, while the network stack (WHATWG URL parser, inet_aton, getaddrinfo) decodes leading-zero octets as octal — the library and the network stack disagree about which host a string names (CVE-2026-69192, advisory CVSS 8.6). Applications that build trust-boundary decisions (e.g. private-IP / SSRF allowlist checks) on Address4.isPrivate() can be tricked into connecting to a different host than the one validated.\n\nNo application code in this repository uses the `ip-address` library, so the octal/decimal confusion has no reachable trust-boundary decision to subvert here. The finding documents the vulnerable pinned version in the agent runtime's dependency tree; it matters only if the SDK (or future code) performs IP-based filtering on attacker-controlled URLs using this library.\n\nAdd an override in agentcore/package.json (\"overrides\": { \"ip-address\": \"^10.3.1\" }) to resolve a patched version in the SDK chain, or track the upstream @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk release that bumps the MCP SDK. No application change is required."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-20",
                  "CVE-2026-69192",
                  "stride:T"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-295",
              "name": "undici 6.24.1 in backend (CVE-2026-109383 dropped TLS callback, not reachable as used)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "undici 6.24.1 in backend (CVE-2026-109383 dropped TLS callback, not reachable as used)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The backend pins `undici` 6.24.1 in `backend/package-lock.json` (transitive via `@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk` and `discord.js`). undici versions 4.0.0–<6.29.1 are affected by CVE-2026-109383 (advisory CVSS 9.1): the Agent dispatcher deep-clones its constructor options via JSON before forwarding them to per-origin pools, silently dropping a caller-supplied `tls.checkServerIdentity` callback — so a TLS peer that a custom certificate check was written to reject is silently accepted if it passes Node's default checks."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The backend pins `undici` 6.24.1 in `backend/package-lock.json` (transitive via `@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk` and `discord.js`). undici versions 4.0.0–<6.29.1 are affected by CVE-2026-109383 (advisory CVSS 9.1): the Agent dispatcher deep-clones its constructor options via JSON before forwarding them to per-origin pools, silently dropping a caller-supplied `tls.checkServerIdentity` callback — so a TLS peer that a custom certificate check was written to reject is silently accepted if it passes Node's default checks.\n\nNo application code creates undici Agents or supplies TLS options, so the dropped-callback path has no caller in this codebase; the finding documents the CVSS-9.1-vulnerable undici pinned in the production dependency tree, relevant only if a future code path configures custom TLS verification through undici.\n\nAdd an npm override in backend/package.json (\"overrides\": { \"undici\": \"^6.29.1\" }) so the claude-agent-sdk/discord.js chains resolve a patched undici, and verify with `npm ls undici`. No code change required today.",
                "markdown": "The backend pins `undici` 6.24.1 in `backend/package-lock.json` (transitive via `@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk` and `discord.js`). undici versions 4.0.0–<6.29.1 are affected by CVE-2026-109383 (advisory CVSS 9.1): the Agent dispatcher deep-clones its constructor options via JSON before forwarding them to per-origin pools, silently dropping a caller-supplied `tls.checkServerIdentity` callback — so a TLS peer that a custom certificate check was written to reject is silently accepted if it passes Node's default checks.\n\nNo application code creates undici Agents or supplies TLS options, so the dropped-callback path has no caller in this codebase; the finding documents the CVSS-9.1-vulnerable undici pinned in the production dependency tree, relevant only if a future code path configures custom TLS verification through undici.\n\nAdd an npm override in backend/package.json (\"overrides\": { \"undici\": \"^6.29.1\" }) so the claude-agent-sdk/discord.js chains resolve a patched undici, and verify with `npm ls undici`. No code change required today."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-295",
                  "CVE-2026-109383",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/295.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-95",
              "name": "Authenticated RCE via eval() of workflow custom transform expression",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Authenticated RCE via eval() of workflow custom transform expression"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The workflow execution engine evaluates attacker-supplied transform expressions with JavaScript eval() without any sanitization or sandboxing. An authenticated user with ordinary 'member' role in an organization can supply a workflow whose action node carries a crafted custom transform expression, execute it, and achieve arbitrary code execution in the backend Node.js process."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The workflow execution engine evaluates attacker-supplied transform expressions with JavaScript eval() without any sanitization or sandboxing. An authenticated user with ordinary 'member' role in an organization can supply a workflow whose action node carries a crafted custom transform expression, execute it, and achieve arbitrary code execution in the backend Node.js process.\n\nFull server-side code execution in the backend process: arbitrary file read/write (demonstrated by writing a marker file via fs.writeFileSync), access to the entire process environment (demonstrated by enumerating process.env), and by extension the Postgres/Redis/S3/AWS credentials held by the process. This compromises all tenants' data.\n\nRemove eval from the transform pipeline. Replace expression evaluation with a restricted, whitelisted transformation DSL or a sandboxed interpreter (e.g. isolated-vm with no host bindings), and validate transform expressions server-side before persisting or executing them. Apply the same treatment to code-artifact-executor.ts:296, which evals node 'code' fields of the same provenance. Ensure workflow canvas node payloads are structurally validated (schema-restricted), not z.array(z.unknown()).",
                "markdown": "The workflow execution engine evaluates attacker-supplied transform expressions with JavaScript eval() without any sanitization or sandboxing. An authenticated user with ordinary 'member' role in an organization can supply a workflow whose action node carries a crafted custom transform expression, execute it, and achieve arbitrary code execution in the backend Node.js process.\n\nFull server-side code execution in the backend process: arbitrary file read/write (demonstrated by writing a marker file via fs.writeFileSync), access to the entire process environment (demonstrated by enumerating process.env), and by extension the Postgres/Redis/S3/AWS credentials held by the process. This compromises all tenants' data.\n\nRemove eval from the transform pipeline. Replace expression evaluation with a restricted, whitelisted transformation DSL or a sandboxed interpreter (e.g. isolated-vm with no host bindings), and validate transform expressions server-side before persisting or executing them. Apply the same treatment to code-artifact-executor.ts:296, which evals node 'code' fields of the same provenance. Ensure workflow canvas node payloads are structurally validated (schema-restricted), not z.array(z.unknown())."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-95",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/95.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-918",
              "name": "Unrestricted server-side fetch (SSRF) with response readback in workflow API-call node",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unrestricted server-side fetch (SSRF) with response readback in workflow API-call node"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The workflow API-call node executes server-side fetches to fully attacker-controlled URLs with no allowlist, private-IP, or scheme restriction. Any authenticated organization member (the lowest privilege role) can point a workflow node at internal targets such as the cloud instance metadata service (169.254.169.254), loopback services, or RFC1910 hosts, set arbitrary request headers, and read the full response body back through the execution result."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The workflow API-call node executes server-side fetches to fully attacker-controlled URLs with no allowlist, private-IP, or scheme restriction. Any authenticated organization member (the lowest privilege role) can point a workflow node at internal targets such as the cloud instance metadata service (169.254.169.254), loopback services, or RFC1910 hosts, set arbitrary request headers, and read the full response body back through the execution result.\n\nAn authenticated low-privilege org member can exfiltrate the application server's cloud instance metadata credentials (AWS IMDS, GCP/Azure equivalents reachable from the host), probe and read internal services (Redis admin, admin panels, Kubernetes endpoints), and pivot those credentials into the cloud account. Demonstrated: arbitrary internal URL + attacker-chosen method/headers + complete response body returned to the caller via the workflow execution result.\n\nRestrict the API-call node's outbound requests: validate the resolved URL (after variable substitution, at execution time) against a denylist of non-routable ranges (loopback, RFC1910, link-local 169.254.0.0/16 including cloud metadata, IPv6 equivalents, and decimal/hex IP encodings), and optionally an allowlist of approved external hosts per organization. Enforce HTTPS-only unless explicitly opted in. Consider blocking or scrubbing user-settable headers that can bypass IMDSv2 protections. If arbitrary outbound HTTP is a deliberate product capability, gate it behind an explicit admin-configured egress policy rather than the default member role.",
                "markdown": "The workflow API-call node executes server-side fetches to fully attacker-controlled URLs with no allowlist, private-IP, or scheme restriction. Any authenticated organization member (the lowest privilege role) can point a workflow node at internal targets such as the cloud instance metadata service (169.254.169.254), loopback services, or RFC1910 hosts, set arbitrary request headers, and read the full response body back through the execution result.\n\nAn authenticated low-privilege org member can exfiltrate the application server's cloud instance metadata credentials (AWS IMDS, GCP/Azure equivalents reachable from the host), probe and read internal services (Redis admin, admin panels, Kubernetes endpoints), and pivot those credentials into the cloud account. Demonstrated: arbitrary internal URL + attacker-chosen method/headers + complete response body returned to the caller via the workflow execution result.\n\nRestrict the API-call node's outbound requests: validate the resolved URL (after variable substitution, at execution time) against a denylist of non-routable ranges (loopback, RFC1910, link-local 169.254.0.0/16 including cloud metadata, IPv6 equivalents, and decimal/hex IP encodings), and optionally an allowlist of approved external hosts per organization. Enforce HTTPS-only unless explicitly opted in. Consider blocking or scrubbing user-settable headers that can bypass IMDSv2 protections. If arbitrary outbound HTTP is a deliberate product capability, gate it behind an explicit admin-configured egress policy rather than the default member role."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-918",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/918.html"
            },
