            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0010",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 10.0,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-21 10:02:52 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/agentcore-superagent-update/super-agent/app-hosting/poc",
              "cwe": "CWE-798",
              "impact": "On a host running this stack: any attacker who can reach port 8081 gets the unauthenticated Traefik dashboard (full routing/service configuration disclosure, ability to observe and manipulate proxies). The docker.sock mounts mean a compromise or SSRF/RCE inside the traefik or app-host-api containers (both reachable from the network) escalates to full host compromise — arbitrary container execution, secret and volume access. The hardcoded, predictable per-tenant JWT secrets allow forging valid tenant tokens (runtime/src/auth.ts falls back to 'poc-secret-change-me' when the env var is unset, so any deployment that relies on the fallback is forgeable).",
              "technical_analysis": "Four independently exploitable patterns in the PoC stack, each verified at its cited line:\n\n1. `/var/run/docker.sock` is bind-mounted into both the `traefik` service and the `app-host-api` service (docker-compose.floci.yml). Both services publish ports to the host (8080/8081 and 3001 respectively), so both are network-reachable processes holding the Docker control socket. Any code execution or SSRF-class issue inside either container — including via the app-host-api's tenant provisioning endpoints — converts directly into arbitrary container execution on the host and access to every other tenant's containers, volumes, and environment secrets.\n\n2. Traefik runs with `--api.insecure=true` and the dashboard entrypoint is published as `\"8081:8080\"`, so the dashboard and its read/write HTTP API are reachable without authentication on all interfaces of the host.\n\n3. `services/app-host-api/src/insforge.ts` lines 54, 64, 74 hardcode per-tenant `jwtSecret` values ('alpha-jwt-secret-poc-change-me', 'beta-jwt-secret-poc-change-me', 'gamma-jwt-secret-poc-change-me'). These secrets sign tenant tokens used by the app-host API; anyone who knows the repo can forge tokens for any tenant.\n\n4. `services/runtime/src/auth.ts:4` falls back to `process.env.JWT_SECRET || 'poc-secret-change-me'`, and `k8s/tenants/*.yaml` ship literal JWT_SECRET values ('alpha-schedule-jwt-secret', 'beta-crm-jwt-secret', 'gamma-dash-jwt-secret') as plain manifest env entries, so deployments either run with a predictable public secret or rely on a fallback that is itself public.",
              "remediation_steps": "Do not mount docker.sock into containers that accept network traffic; use a socket proxy with restricted operations, or a container-runtime API with least privilege. Disable --api.insecure (or bind the dashboard to 127.0.0.1 behind auth). Replace every hardcoded tenant JWT secret with per-deployment generated secrets sourced from a secret store; remove the runtime fallback default and fail fast when JWT_SECRET is unset. In the k8s manifests, move JWT_SECRET values into Secrets (the schedule-app already uses a secretRef — extend that pattern to all apps).",
              "counterevidence": "The whole tree sits under app-hosting/poc/ with README/ARCHITECTURE framing it as a demonstration; docker-compose uses providers.docker.exposedbydefault=false (only labelled routes exposed); the tenant-alpha schedule app pulls JWT_SECRET from a secretRef rather than a manifest literal, showing awareness of secret management; and docker.sock mounts plus the insecure dashboard are common (if risky) patterns in local docker-compose labs. However none of these are enforcement controls: the traefik dashboard is still reachable on 0.0.0.0:8081, the JWT secrets are still literal defaults, and the API container still gets full host-level docker control.",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "confidence_rationale": "All cited lines verified statically; no running instance was available to confirm exposure, and the PoC labelling plus k8s secretRef usage suggest these manifests may not ship to production. Local-only assessment per mandate.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Evidence this PoC stack (or the k8s manifests) is deployed beyond a local demo — e.g. reachable dashboard or API on a shared host — would raise severity to high; confirmation that the PoC is exclusively a disposable local lab with no shared access would lower it to low.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Verify docker-compose.floci.yml mounts /var/run/docker.sock into both the traefik and app-host-api services and runs Traefik with --api.insecure=true, publishing the dashboard on host port 8081.\n2. Verify app-host-api/src/insforge.ts:54/64/74 hardcodes tenant JWT secrets ('alpha-jwt-secret-poc-change-me' etc.) and runtime/src/auth.ts:4 falls back to 'poc-secret-change-me'.\n3. On any host running the stack: `curl http://<host>:8081/api/http/routers` returns the unauthenticated dashboard API; inside either container, `docker -H unix:///var/run/docker.sock run ...` executes arbitrary containers on the host; with a known tenant secret, forge a JWT for any tenant/app identity.\n4. None of these steps were executed against a live host (local-only assessment); each control path is verified from source.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
